  <h2 id="degree-levels">What are the behavioral psychology degree levels?</h2>
  <p>A behavioral psychology degree comes in four levels, and each opens different doors. An associate degree takes two years and leads to support roles. A bachelor's takes four and opens entry-level professional roles. A master's takes about two and unlocks applied specialist roles. A PhD takes five to seven years and leads to research, teaching, and licensed practice. The table below gives you the quick version; the sections after it go deeper.</p>

  <div class="table-wrap"><table class="data-table">
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Degree level</th><th scope="col">Typical duration</th><th scope="col">Focus</th><th scope="col">Career doors it opens</th><th scope="col">Typical cost range</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><th scope="row">Associate</th><td>2 years</td><td>Introductory psychology, general education, basic research literacy</td><td>Entry-level support roles; stepping stone to a bachelor's</td><td>$7,000 - $25,000 total</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Bachelor's</th><td>4 years (2 more after an associate)</td><td>Core psychology theory, statistics, research methods, electives in behavior</td><td>Research assistant, HR associate, training coordinator, sales and marketing roles</td><td>$30,000 - $120,000 total</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Master's (MS or MA)</th><td>2 years full time; 12 - 18 months accelerated</td><td>Advanced behavior analysis, experimental design, statistics, applied practicum</td><td>Organizational behavior specialist, program evaluator, UX researcher, community program manager</td><td>$25,000 - $90,000 total</td></tr>
