  <p>Start with the foundations, then match a degree level to your target career. Read <a href="/what-is-behavioral-psychology/">what behavioral psychology is</a> to confirm the field fits, pick the level that unlocks the roles you want, and check the <a href="/careers/">careers guide</a> before you commit to a program. Then read the detailed guide for your level: the <a href="/degrees/ms-behavioral-psychology/">MS path</a>, the broader <a href="/degrees/masters-in-behavioral-psychology/">master's overview</a>, the <a href="/degrees/phd-in-behavioral-psychology/">PhD route</a>, or <a href="/degrees/online-behavioral-psychology-degree/">online options</a>. A deliberate choice now saves you years and thousands of dollars later.</p>
